// ═══ synchronized: BLOCKED state ═══
synchronized (obj) { /* critical section */ }
// Contended threads: Thread.State.BLOCKED
// jstack: "BLOCKED on com.bank.AccountService@0x7f..."
// ThreadMXBean: findMonitorDeadlockedThreads() detects!

// ═══ ReentrantLock: WAITING state (not BLOCKED!) ═══
ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
lock.lock();  // if contended → AQS → LockSupport.park() → WAITING!
// Contended threads: Thread.State.WAITING (or TIMED_WAITING for tryLock(timeout))
// jstack:
//   "WAITING (parking)"
//   at jdk.internal.misc.Unsafe.park(Native Method)
//   at java.util.concurrent.locks.LockSupport.park(LockSupport.java:194)
//   at java.util.concurrent.locks.AbstractQueuedSynchronizer.parkAndCheckInterrupt(...)
// NOT "BLOCKED"! Looks like the thread is just waiting, not contending!

// ═══ WHY THE DIFFERENCE? ═══
// synchronized: JVM manages the monitor → BLOCKED state
// ReentrantLock: AQS manages the queue → park() → WAITING state
// ThreadMXBean.findDeadlockedThreads(): finds BOTH types!
// But: "BLOCKED" count in thread dump = only synchronized contention!

// ═══ PROFILING TOOLS ═══
// 1. JFR (JDK Flight Recorder):
// jcmd <PID> JFR.start duration=60s filename=locks.jfr
// In JDK Mission Control: "Thread Park" events
// Shows: park duration, blocker class, stack trace!
// Filter: jdk.ThreadPark events where duration > 1ms!

// 2. async-profiler (lock contention mode):
// ./profiler.sh -d 30 -e lock -o flamegraph -f lock_flame.html <PID>
// Flame graph: width = time spent WAITING for locks!
// Tall stacks = deep call chains holding locks!
// Wide methods = contention hotspots!

// 3. JFR + JMC analysis:
// "Lock Instances" tab → most contended locks (by total wait time!)
// "Hot Methods" tab → methods holding locks longest!
// "Thread" tab → per-thread park time!

// ═══ WHAT Unsafe.park TIME MEANS ═══
// Thread spending time in park() = WAITING for a lock = CONTENTION!
// NOT doing useful work! Not computing! Just waiting!
// High park() time → reduce lock scope, switch to finer locking, use lock-free!
